1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 17: VHF antennas on a roof

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:20,392
YA-NEC course, lesson 17: VHF antennas on a roof. Examples: the DK7ZB 7-element EME Yagi for 144 MHz, from an MMANA file.

3
00:00:20,442 --> 00:00:30,846
In free space: 28.2 + j0.8 ohm (a 28 ohm design), 13.35 dBi, F/B 23.9 dB.

4
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:46,607
Calculators, Tools, 'Antenna on a roof (slab)'.

5
00:00:46,657 --> 00:00:55,220
Roof slab 15 m above the ground. The model is at z = 0, so the tool warns: the antenna touches the slab.

6
00:00:55,270 --> 00:01:04,898
'Height to add to the model': 3 m above the slab.

7
00:01:04,948 --> 00:01:09,898
18 m from the ground in total, 8.65 wavelengths.

8
00:01:09,948 --> 00:01:33,562
'Compute both cases (NEC)'.

9
00:01:33,612 --> 00:01:50,641
HF case, the real ground under the building at 18 m: 19.28 dBi at 1.7 degrees elevation. VHF case, the concrete slab under the antenna (ITU-R P.2040): 18.19 dBi at 9.1 degrees.

10
00:01:50,691 --> 00:02:00,964
The impedance hardly changes - 28.2 or 28.1 ohm - but the elevation of the main lobe does: 1.7 or 9.1 degrees, and 1 dB of gain.

11
00:02:01,014 --> 00:02:11,165
Reality sits in between: the higher the frequency and the larger the roof, the more the slab case applies. For EME at moonrise it matters.

12
00:02:11,215 --> 00:02:40,740
Case 'VHF/UHF: slab', 'Open as model', Patterns.

13
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:48,607
Elevation over the slab: the lobe is lifted from the horizon, with the first reflection lobes above it.

14
00:02:48,657 --> 00:03:00,079
The ground menu also has the two concrete slab entries (VHF-UHF ITU-R P.2040, HF Perotoni 2019), whose values follow the frequency.

15
00:03:00,129 --> 00:03:12,823
A roof is not free space and not plain ground: two cases, side by side, in one click. Next lesson: VHF stacks, noise and G/T. 73 de IC8TEM
